{"cuisine/yoshoku":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/yoshoku","n":"Yoshoku","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Japan","intro":[["In Japanese cuisine, yōshoku refers to a style of Western-influenced cooking which originated during the Meiji Restoration. These are primarily Japanized forms of European dishes, often featuring Western names, and usually written in katakana. It is an example of fusion cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"yoshoku"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoshoku"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Japanese Soul Cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Tadashi Ono and Harris Salat, 2013","note":"Japanese comfort food such as curry, tonkatsu and korokke, including Western-influenced dishes.","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/218238/japanese-soul-cooking-by-tadashi-ono-and-harris-salat/","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781607743521"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":9,"links":[["Chicken katsu","dish/chicken-katsu"],["Cream stew","dish/cream-stew"],["Doria (food)","dish/doria-food"],["Hayashi rice","dish/hayashi-rice"],["Menchi-katsu","dish/menchi-katsu"],["Naporitan","dish/naporitan"],["Tonkatsu","dish/tonkatsu"],["Toruko rice","dish/toruko-rice"],["Volga rice","dish/volga-rice"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Panko","ingredient/panko"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Pilaf","ingredient/pilaf"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Spaghetti","ingredient/spaghetti"],["Tonkatsu","ingredient/tonkatsu"],["Fried chicken","ingredient/fried-chicken"],["Japanese rice","ingredient/japanese-rice"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Roux","ingredient/roux"],["Béchamel","ingredient/bechamel"],["Béchamel sauce","ingredient/bechamel-sauce"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Demi-glace","ingredient/demi-glace"],["Mushroom","ingredient/mushroom"],["Red wine","ingredient/red-wine"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"],["Agaricus bisporus","ingredient/agaricus-bisporus"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a9/Korokke.jpg/500px-Korokke.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Pieria(Uploader and Photographer)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korokke.jpg"}},"cuisine/swiss-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/swiss-cuisine","n":"Swiss cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Switzerland","intro":[["Swiss cuisine is an ensemble of national, regional, and local dishes, consisting of the ingredients, recipes, and cooking techniques developed in Switzerland or assimilated from other cultures, particularly neighboring countries. The diversity and comprehensiveness of Swiss gastronomy reflect Swiss linguistic, cultural, and geographical diversity. The climate of Switzerland allows for a large variety of terroirs and, therefore, a wide range of indigenous foods, from simple cereals and vegetables to refined products such as cheese and wine."],["Switzerland is historically an agricultural country, with many regions being isolated from each other by the Alps. Therefore, one of the main characteristics of Swiss cuisine is its simplicity, with many dishes made up of few but hearty ingredients, often of dairy origin. Swiss cuisine evolved dramatically during the last centuries. Probably the most significant changes occurred after colonization of the Americas and the introduction of now-widely used ingredients such as potatoes, maize, and cocoa. The increase in purchasing power and a certain homogenization of taste have allowed the emergence of some emblematic national dishes such as fondue and rösti."],["The most well-known Swiss products include cheese, meat specialties, wine, and chocolate. Switzerland is particularly renowned for its wide variety of cheeses and cured meats, which reflect the country’s regional traditions. Some traditional Swiss food products are protected by a geographical indication (AOP) and are exported worldwide. Over the centuries, Switzerland has developed a strong food industry, particularly associated with dairy and chocolate production, which plays an important role in the national economy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"swiss-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swiss_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Helvetic Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"helvetickitchen.com","note":"Andie Pilot's blog of Swiss home cooking and baking, with notes on Swiss cookbooks.","url":"https://www.helvetickitchen.com/recipes/retrospective","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Cuisine Helvetica","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cuisinehelvetica.com","note":"Heddi Nieuwsma's Swiss recipes and food culture from her life in Switzerland.","url":"https://cuisinehelvetica.com/swiss-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Simply Swiss","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Andie Pilot, 2025","note":"Traditional Swiss recipes from the Helvetic Kitchen author, published by Helvetiq.","url":"https://helvetiq.com/ch_en/simply-swiss","suggested":true,"isbn":"9783039640799"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":20,"links":[["Älplermagronen","dish/alplermagronen"],["Basler Läckerli","dish/basler-lackerli"],["Birnbrot","dish/birnbrot"],["Bündnerfleisch","dish/bundnerfleisch"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Carac (pastry)","dish/carac-pastry"],["Cervelat","dish/cervelat"],["Cholera (food)","dish/cholera-food"],["Egg puffs and soup pearls","dish/egg-puffs-and-soup-pearls"],["Landjäger","dish/landjager"],["Leberkäse","dish/leberkase"],["Raclette","dish/raclette"],["Riz Casimir","dish/riz-casimir"],["Schenkele","dish/schenkele"],["Spätzle","dish/spatzle"],["Swiss sausages and cured meats","dish/swiss-sausages-and-cured-meats"],["Wähe","dish/wahe"],["Zopf","dish/zopf"],["Zuger Kirschtorte","dish/zuger-kirschtorte"],["Zürcher Geschnetzeltes","dish/zurcher-geschnetzeltes"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Candied fruit","ingredient/candied-fruit"],["Hazelnut","ingredient/hazelnut"],["Kirsch","ingredient/kirsch"],["Apple","ingredient/apple"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Common fig","ingredient/common-fig"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Pear","ingredient/pear"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Dried meat","ingredient/dried-meat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2b/Stand_mit_Schweizer_Nahrungsmitteln.jpg/500px-Stand_mit_Schweizer_Nahrungsmitteln.jpg","w":500,"h":348,"by":"Sandstein","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stand_mit_Schweizer_Nahrungsmitteln.jpg"}},"cuisine/welsh-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/welsh-cuisine","n":"Welsh cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United Kingdom","intro":[["Welsh cuisine encompasses the cooking styles, traditions and recipes associated with Wales. While there are many dishes that can be considered Welsh due to their ingredients and/or history, dishes such as cawl, Welsh rarebit, laverbread, Welsh cakes, bara brith, and Glamorgan sausage have all been regarded as symbols of Welsh food. Some variation in dishes exists across the country, with notable differences existing in the Gower Peninsula, a historically isolated rural area which developed self-sufficiency in food production (see Cuisine of Gower)."],["While some culinary practices and dishes have been imported from other parts of Britain, uniquely Welsh cuisine grew principally from the lives of Welsh working people, largely as a result of their isolation from outside culinary influences and the need to produce food based on the limited ingredients they could produce or afford."],["Due to the nature of the landscape, sheep farming in Wales is well-suited and extensive, with lamb and mutton being the meats most traditionally associated with the country. Beef and dairy cattle are also raised widely, and there is a strong fishing culture. Fisheries and commercial fishing are common and seafood features widely in Welsh cuisine."],["Vegetables, beyond cabbages and leeks, were historically rare. The leek has been a national symbol of Wales for at least 400 years and Shakespeare refers to the Welsh custom of wearing a leek in Henry V."],["Since the 1970s the number of restaurants and gastropubs in Wales has increased significantly and by 2024 there were six Michelin-starred restaurants located in the country."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"welsh-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Welsh_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Baking with Granny: Welsh recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"bakingwithgranny.co.uk","note":"Traditional Welsh baking recipes such as Welsh cakes and bara brith.","url":"https://bakingwithgranny.co.uk/category/recipe/welsh/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Flavours of Wales: The Baking Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Gilli Davies, Huw Jones","note":"Welsh baking recipes by a long-standing Welsh food writer, with photography by Huw Jones.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Bara brith","dish/bara-brith"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Zante currant","ingredient/zante-currant"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Welsh cooking","count":5,"links":[["Cuisine of Carmarthenshire","cuisine/cuisine-of-carmarthenshire"],["Cuisine of Ceredigion","cuisine/cuisine-of-ceredigion"],["Cuisine of Gower","cuisine/cuisine-of-gower"],["Cuisine of Monmouthshire","cuisine/cuisine-of-monmouthshire"],["Cuisine of Pembrokeshire","cuisine/cuisine-of-pembrokeshire"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/97/Welsh_cakes2.jpg/500px-Welsh_cakes2.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Moochocoogle at English Wikipedia","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Welsh_cakes2.jpg"}},"ingredient/white-wine":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/white-wine","n":"White wine","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 7 dishes across 8 cuisines","intro":[["White wine is a wine that is fermented without undergoing the process of maceration, which involves prolonged contact between the juice and the grape skins, seeds, and pulp. The wine color can be straw-yellow, yellow-green, or yellow-gold. It is produced by the alcoholic fermentation of the noncolored pulp of grapes, which may have a skin of any color. White wine has existed for at least 4,000 years."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-white-wine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_wine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":8,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"]]],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Flummery","dish/flummery"]]],["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine",[["Androlla","dish/androlla"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Chakapuli","dish/chakapuli"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Pizza dolce di Beridde","dish/pizza-dolce-di-beridde"]]],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine",[["Pizza dolce di Beridde","dish/pizza-dolce-di-beridde"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","dish/ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Clam","ingredient/clam"],["Clams","ingredient/clams"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Crab meat","ingredient/crab-meat"],["Crayfish as food","ingredient/crayfish-as-food"],["Crustacean","ingredient/crustacean"],["Langoustine","ingredient/langoustine"],["Lobster meat","ingredient/lobster-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/7/71/White_Wine_Glas.jpg","w":500,"h":998,"by":"André Karwath aka Aka","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Wine_Glas.jpg"}},"ingredient/green-peas":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/green-peas","n":"Green peas","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredi